{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c465","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0aba0-7282-7543-b2c1-245c5391567d"},"sequence":141,"recorded_at":1780531400000277,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"3cb3b2c5-dd67-49c0-a2b1-aadb0810f3b1","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"3cb3b2c5-dd67-49c0-a2b1-aadb0810f3b1","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"3cb3b2c5-dd67-49c0-a2b1-aadb0810f3b1"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0aba0-f97e-77f0-8762-f3c60a142ecd"},"task_id":"01a0aba0-f97e-77f0-8762-f3c60a142ecd","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0aba0-f97e-77f0-8762-f3c60a142ecd","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-4-1\",\n  \"command\": \"D=\\\"/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260909-2ez769\\\"\\nfor f in TASK-260909-2ez769_reviewer-cr5.md TASK-260909-2ez769_rework-rev5.md TASK-260909-2ez769_reviewer-cr6.md TASK-260909-2ez769_rework-rev6.md; do echo \\\"===== $f =====\\\"; cat \\\"$D/$f\\\"; echo; done\",\n  \"description\": \"Read second batch of task directives\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"===== TASK-260909-2ez769_reviewer-cr5.md =====\\nIndependently review Change Request revision 5 of TASK-260909-2ez769 (task_delta on managed checkpoint a89328ca85a613abde39660f4be749a8fcf57903, candidate tree a823eb20be497c3fa0510e7da4d3506ddb284431, published by RUN-260916-c0f186). This is the rework after the CR4 changes-requested verdict (TASK-260909-2ez769_review-verdict-rev4.md, evidence zip rev4); read that verdict, the CR4 logbook, the producer outcome TASK-260909-2ez769_results-rev5.md with TASK-260909-2ez769_producer-evidence-rev5.tar.gz and TASK-260909-2ez769_exclusion-handoff.md, the CR patch and validation log, and the exact immutable CR bytes. Superseded reviewer briefs (reviewer, reviewer-current-base, reviewer-after-rev3, reviewer-after-rev3-current) are historical; this brief governs.\\n\\nThe producer ported the CR4 probes under new names (TestSurvivingReaderConvergesInterruptedApply, TestSurvivingReaderConvergesInterruptedRollback, TestFirstOpenPreservesHeldLock, TestConcurrentOpenAttachesToExistingLock) and claims a marker admission barrier in internal/hosttrust/joint.go and transact.go (markerPresent, convergeLocked, lockForConvergedRead), O_CREAT|O_EXCL / CREATE_NEW lock creation (FileSystem.CreateExclusive) with a 500ms verification budget, seven build-tagged Windows custody tests, TestApplyV4BackupIsClassifiedConfigCopy, a row-21 handoff with notes on seven owning tasks, 19 of 21 rows driven (rows 15 and 21 explicitly not driven), 25+1 hosttrust and 4+1 config plants killed with a neutral SURVIVED control and one documented converge-ignore survivor by the staged-marker backstop. These are producer claims requiring independent verification; in particular decide whether the shared-reader release-then-reacquire path leaves a window in which a second writer stages a new marker between the shared release and the exclusive acquisition, and whether the 500ms lock verification retry can admit a replaced inode. Verify the three CR4 findings against production entry points, RED-first evidence and your own isolated reproduction:\\nF1 (surviving-process admission): port or rerun TestReviewerCrashBetweenConfigAndGeneration and TestReviewerRollbackCrashSurvivingReader on the exact candidate; the surviving LoadCoherent must refuse or converge, never admit old generation 3/4; confirm the pending joint-commit marker is a general admission barrier across WithSharedSnapshot/readSnapshotLocked, readCommitted, authorization boundaries and mutation transactions, that stale requests are still refused (never silently refreshed), and that TestApplyV4CrashConvergesGeneration now asserts convergence with fatal expectations instead of returning silently on Load/Configuration/Open/ReadSnapshot errors. Cover apply and rollback, surviving and reopened processes, marker cleanup/error ordering. Distinguish tested refusal from proven recovery.\\nF2 (first-open lock replacement): rerun TestReviewerCrossProcessLockInitialization; with the child paused after the absence observation and the parent holding the lock, the child must end up on the same inode and must not return while the parent holds it; confirm initialization is atomic and non-replacing through production Open, that existing-lock contention is tested, and read the Windows initialization path for the same rule (report Windows execution honestly as unverified where no runtime ran).\\nF3 (evidence and ownership): native Windows DACL tests must exist as real build-tagged test code driving the production custody entries for owner-only acceptance, other-principal refusal and reparse handling; without a Windows runtime, row 15 is implementation present / runtime unverified, never a pass; config-backup contents must be classified against the applicable custody contract rather than profile-ACL inheritance; row 21 must carry a precise handoff naming each downstream owning task, API and test obligation (check the notes the producer wrote on those tasks) and be counted as not consumer-driven.\\n\\nRetain the CR3 repairs (stale generation refusal, same/separate-Store revocation contention) and the corrected config mutation accounting; re-derive the 21-row AC table from source and named tests, counting driven/bounded/failing rows honestly; check that every claimed exit comes from a real command status and that the harmless SURVIVED control was applied through the same harness; no compile-failure, unapplied or empty-selection kills. Confirm the parked RPC artifacts and control-root backup hashes are untouched and that no candidate path overlaps the RPC manifest; confirm checkpointed SSH/peer predecessors are preserved and no RPC transport was implemented.\\n\\nMethod: isolated immutable probes only (throwaway clone or extracted tree), never mutate the live Story worktree, index, branch or HEAD; no product edits, commits, checkpoints or integration; Curator go-testing-tools skill; all CI local. Attach TASK-260909-2ez769_review-verdict-rev5.md and TASK-260909-2ez769_review-evidence-rev5.zip, complete the live merged checklist, then accept_cr(TASK-260909-2ez769, revision=5, evidence=TASK-260909-2ez769_review-verdict-rev5.md) or hand off changes requested with P1/P2 classes and repeat-of named. Model: codex gpt-6-astra low. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board.\\n\\n===== TASK-260909-2ez769_rework-rev5.md =====\\nRework the credentials/Config4 candidate of TASK-260909-2ez769 after the CR5 independent changes-requested verdict by RUN-260916-f8925e (Astra low). Read TASK-260909-2ez769_review-verdict-rev5.md, TASK-260909-2ez769_review-logbook-rev5.md and materialize TASK-260909-2ez769_review-evidence-rev5.zip (probe TestReviewerRestoreCannotOverwriteConvergedCommit, logs, mutation replay). Base is still the managed checkpoint a89328ca85a613abde39660f4be749a8fcf57903; CR5 candidate tree a823eb20be497c3fa0510e7da4d3506ddb284431 in the managed Story worktree .temp/STORY-260830-1kiyj6/worktree — continue from it. Preserve the checkpointed SSH/peer leaves, the parked RPC artifacts and the control-root backup with their recorded hashes; no RPC transport; no manual commits; no direct .task-board edits; no self-acceptance. Superseded briefs (producer, rework-rev3, rework-rev4) are scope context only; this brief governs.\\n\\nThe CR5 finding is the third consecutive verdict in the crash/coherence transaction family (CR3 crash-transaction, CR4 surviving readers, CR5 unlocked error compensation): each rework closed the witnessed seam and left the next mutation path outside the lock. Do not fix restoreSourceAfterJointFailure as one more seam. First build the instrument that closes the class: a write-path census of every code path that creates, replaces, removes or renames config.json, its backups, trust.json, the pending joint-commit marker, the lock file and the credential files — apply, rollback, restore-after-joint-failure, Recover/convergeLocked, Initialize, JointCommit leftover handling, Enroll/Rotate/Revoke/MarkRetiring, backup publication, and any helper (writeTempReplace, replaceDurably, secureStaged, CreateExclusive) — as a table with, per row: the production call site, the lock state it requires (exclusive with converged intent / shared fast path / none), the intent revalidation it performs before writing (current generation, marker state, exact preview/source), the positive test, the interleaving negative (a surviving reader or another writer completing or superseding the operation between the failure and the compensation), and the narrowing plant. Enforce the census with a gate that fails when a filesystem mutation of the pair happens outside a held exclusive authorization: either a static gate over the hosttrust/config packages (every caller of the replace/remove helpers must be reachable only from a lock-held context, with a control plant that proves the gate sees a new unlocked caller) or a runtime assertion in the helpers keyed to the lock holder, with a behavioral test for each row. Attach the census as TASK-260909-2ez769_write-path-census-rev6.md.\\n\\nThen repair F1 against the census: error compensation after a failed JointCommit (apply and rollback) must run inside the same authorization transaction or reacquire the exclusive lock and revalidate the exact intent (pending marker state, current generation, configuration bytes/digest) before choosing a converged outcome; if another process completed, superseded or converged the operation, the compensation must not restore the old configuration — it refuses or adopts the converged state with the generation change intact. Port TestReviewerRestoreCannotOverwriteConvergedCommit RED first (the seam that turns trust.json to 0644 so custody validation refuses the trust commit, then a surviving LoadCoherent converges to Config4/generation 4, then the failed writer resumes), keep it as a committed test, and add deterministic production tests for apply and rollback trust-commit failures racing (a) a surviving reader, (b) another writer, (c) a held boundary. Keep the CR4 repairs green (surviving-reader convergence, first-open lock preservation, stale-generation refusal, revocation contention) and re-run the original seven reviewer probes. Note the reviewer bound on verifyLockFile's 500ms retry (custody rechecked, inode not pinned): document it as a stated bound or pin the inode, but do not claim protection against an owner replacing lock files externally unless you implement it.\\n\\nRetain rows 15 and 21 exactly as CR5 accounted them (Windows native tests present, runtime unverified; matcher-only, consumer handoff persisted); do not inflate them. Update the 21-row AC matrix with actual calls and named tests; rerun the hosttrust and config mutation batteries on the exact final source with per-plant raw logs, a neutral SURVIVED control applied through the same harness, exits from subprocess return codes, and no compile-failure/unapplied/empty-selection kills. Full configured local validation before the normal producer handoff; no hosted CI. Read the Curator-managed go-testing-tools skill first. Leave the candidate UNCOMMITTED for the handoff snapshot. Attach TASK-260909-2ez769_results-rev6.md, TASK-260909-2ez769_write-path-census-rev6.md and TASK-260909-2ez769_producer-evidence-rev6.tar.gz, then hand off through task-board and exit promptly. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be codex gpt-6-astra low.\\n\\n===== TASK-260909-2ez769_reviewer-cr6.md =====\\nIndependently review Change Request revision 6 of TASK-260909-2ez769 (task_delta on managed checkpoint a89328ca85a613abde39660f4be749a8fcf57903, candidate tree b0bb3d9d600d4c323aa12480b3285512f96167c7, published by RUN-260916-44fc4f, 55 changed paths). This is the rework after the CR5 changes-requested verdict (TASK-260909-2ez769_review-verdict-rev5.md, review-evidence-rev5.zip, review-logbook-rev5.md): read that verdict, the producer outcome TASK-260909-2ez769_results-rev6.md, the census TASK-260909-2ez769_write-path-census-rev6.md, TASK-260909-2ez769_producer-evidence-rev6.tar.gz, the rev6 patch and validation log, and the exact immutable CR bytes. Superseded reviewer briefs (reviewer-cr5 and earlier) are historical; this brief governs.\\n\\nCR3, CR4 and CR5 were one family — a config/trust pair mutation outside the authorization transaction — so review the instrument first: the census must enumerate every production path that creates, replaces, removes or renames config.toml, its backups, trust.json, the pending joint-commit marker, the lock file and the credential files (the producer claims 12 rows) with lock state, intent revalidation, positive test, interleaving negative and narrowing plant; verify completeness against the source with your own walk (every caller of writeTempReplace, replaceDurably, secureStaged, CreateExclusive, os.Rename/Remove/Create in internal/config and internal/hosttrust), and verify that TestWritePathCensusGate actually sees a new unlocked caller (plant your own rogue caller in an isolated copy in a shape the producer's three control plants did not use — a new file, a method receiver, a var-bound helper — and confirm the gate fails). An undriven or uncensused row is a finding.\\n\\nThen verify the CR5 F1 repair: JointCommit now takes the restore closure and compensates under the same exclusive hold (compensateLocked: marker re-read and pin-compared, configuration hash equal to the replacement pin, restore, restored bytes re-read against the source pin, marker abort; committed trust deliberately not re-read), the unlocked restoreSourceAfterJointFailure is deleted, ErrJointReplacementDurable reports a clean abort and ErrJointCompensationFailed reports failed compensation with the marker kept. Rerun TestReviewerRestoreCannotOverwriteConvergedCommit on the exact candidate (the producer says its seam now blocks on flock; confirm that the final state can no longer be the CR5 failure and that the port TestApplyV4CompensationCannotOverwriteConvergedCommit asserts the final state), and probe the six compensation race tests for apply and rollback against a surviving reader, another writer and a held boundary; decide whether \\\"never re-read committed trust\\\" can abort a bump that actually became durable and whether that is a safe refusal or a silent loss. Keep the seven original reviewer probes green.\\n\\nRetain rows 15 and 21 exactly as CR5 accounted them (Windows native tests present, runtime unverified; matcher-only, consumer handoff persisted); re-derive the 21-row AC table from source and named tests; check that the hosttrust (29+1 kills, neutral SURVIVED) and config batteries report exits from subprocess return codes with per-plant raw logs and no compile-failure/unapplied/empty-selection kills; confirm RPC backup 15/15 and parked 11/11 hashes and zero overlap with the candidate; confirm checkpointed SSH/peer predecessors are preserved and no RPC transport was implemented.\\n\\nMethod: isolated immutable probes only, never mutate the live Story worktree, index, branch or HEAD; no product edits, commits, checkpoints or integration; Curator go-testing-tools skill; all CI local. Attach TASK-260909-2ez769_review-verdict-rev6.md and TASK-260909-2ez769_review-evidence-rev6.zip, complete the live merged checklist, then accept_cr(TASK-260909-2ez769, revision=6, evidence=TASK-260909-2ez769_review-verdict-rev6.md) or hand off changes requested with P1/P2 classes and repeat-of named. Model: codex gpt-6-astra low. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board.\\n\\n===== TASK-260909-2ez769_rework-rev6.md =====\\nRework the credentials/Config4 candidate of TASK-260909-2ez769 after the CR6 independent changes-requested verdict by RUN-260916-c3e675 (Astra low)